FRM.registerQuestions(42, [{"id":"645","page":361,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"LIBOR."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Fed funds rate."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Repo rate."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Treasury rate."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Donald Gregg is a senior professor of economics at the University of Vikings. He has authored various books on the subject of macroeconomics, financial instruments, and derivatives. He is famous for conducting a bi-yearly informative seminar where he delivers his analysis on finance-related topics. In his last seminar, he said that the government also borrows funds from public institutions in exchange for their guarantee to return the funds with interest. These transactions are considered risk-free as governments are not likely to default. Which of the following rates do governments use to borrow funds denominated in their own currency?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Treasury rate is the interest rate that the government pays to borrow money from investors. This borrowing is done through the issuance of treasury bills and treasury bonds. These are considered risk-free financial instruments as they are backed by the full faith and credit of the government. The government sells these securities to investors to raise funds. The Treasury rate is determined through auctions conducted by the Department of the Treasury. The rate is influenced by various factors including the overall demand for these securities, the economic outlook, and the monetary policy stance of the central bank. The Treasury rate serves as a benchmark for other interest rates in the economy as it is considered the risk-free rate of return."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. LIBOR (London Interbank Offered Rate) is a benchmark interest rate at which major global banks lend to one another in the international interbank market for short-term loans. It does not represent the rate at which governments borrow funds."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. The Fed funds rate, set by the Federal Reserve, is the interest rate at which depository institutions lend reserve balances to other depository institutions overnight on an uncollateralized basis. While it influences other interest rates and thus borrowing costs, it's not directly used as a borrowing rate by governments."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The Repo (Repurchase Agreement) rate refers to the discount rate at which a central bank repurchases government securities from commercial banks, aiming to regulate money supply in the economy. It's not directly related to government borrowing from public institutions."}],"remember":["Government borrowing through treasury securities is a key mechanism for raising funds to finance budget deficits.","The Treasury rate serves as a benchmark for other interest rates in the economy.","Government securities are considered risk-free due to the backing of the government's credit.","Central banks play a crucial role in regulating money supply through various tools like repo rates.","Understanding different interest rates like LIBOR, Fed funds rate, and Repo rate is essential for comprehending the financial markets."]},{"id":"648","page":362,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Treasury rate."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Fed funds rate."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Repo rate."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It cannot be determined."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Xiaojun Lee is the treasury manager at the Atlanta Small Business Bank. She works in a team that supervises all the branches of the banks in Atlanta. Her core responsibility is to look after treasury transactions and to make sure the bank, at all time, meets its reserve requirements with the Federal Reserve. Today, Lee has analyzed that the bank will fall short $200 million from its reserve requirements. In order to avoid penalties, the bank must borrow some funds from another bank. Which of the following rate must Lee use as the reference rate for borrowing $200 million overnight?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Fed funds rate is the rate that banks use for overnight borrowing or lending of surplus funds to meet the Federal Reserve's reserve requirements. This rate is crucial for banks to maintain their reserve requirements and avoid penalties. In this scenario, since the Atlanta Small Business Bank is short of its reserve requirements, it needs to borrow funds overnight. Therefore, the Fed funds rate is the appropriate reference rate for this transaction."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. The Treasury rate is the yield on a U.S. government debt obligation (a U.S. Treasury note or bond) that is considered risk-free as it is backed by America's full faith and credit. It does not apply to interbank lending, which is what Lee needs for meeting the reserve requirements."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The Repo rate, or repurchase rate, refers to the interest rate at which commercial banks borrow short-term funds from a central bank by selling securities with an agreement to repurchase them at a later date. This does not apply in this scenario as Lee needs to borrow from another bank, not from the central bank. <"}],"remember":["Reserve Requirements: Regulations set by the Federal Reserve that require banks to hold a certain amount of funds in reserve to ensure liquidity and stability in the banking system.","Interbank Lending: The borrowing and lending of funds between banks to manage their reserve requirements and maintain liquidity.","Overnight Borrowing: Short-term borrowing or lending of funds that occurs overnight to meet immediate funding needs.","Risk-Free Rate: The rate of return on an investment with no risk of financial loss, often represented by the Treasury rate for U.S. government securities."]},{"id":"649","page":363,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"LIBOR."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Fed funds rate."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Repo rate."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Treasury rate."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Mohan Das is the treasury manager of a bank based in Frankfurt. He is responsible for looking at the bank’s treasury operations and the compliance unit of the bank closely supervises his department. Today, Das is informed by the front office that the bank has to disburse a large fund to an institutional client which they believe will affect the bank's reserves with the central bank. The management suggested borrowing the funds from another bank to meet the central bank’s reserve requirements, but he argues that the bank, instead, should sell its securities to another bank with the promise to purchase the securities back at a higher price. Which of the following interest rates is the manager most likely to use for the given transaction?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Repo Rate, also known as the Repurchase Agreement rate, is the interest rate that Das is most likely referring to in this scenario. The Repo Rate is a borrowing rate used by financial institutions when they sell their securities for a certain price and agree to buy them back at a later date for a higher price. This type of transaction is known as a 'repurchase agreement' or 'repo'. In a repo transaction, the seller is effectively borrowing money and using the securities as collateral, while the buyer is lending money and has the security of the collateral. The difference between the sale price and the repurchase price represents the interest on the loan, which is the Repo Rate. This rate is used as a short-term monetary policy instrument by central banks to control the money supply in the economy. In the given scenario, Das is suggesting a repo transaction to raise the necessary funds without borrowing from another bank, which aligns with the definition and use of the Repo Rate."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. LIBOR (London Interbank Offered Rate) is the interest rate at which banks offer to lend funds (in marketable size) to other banks in the London interbank market. It does not directly relate to repurchase agreements as suggested by Das."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. The Fed funds rate refers to the interest rate at which depository institutions lend reserve balances to other depository institutions overnight on an uncollateralized basis. While it does involve interbank lending, it doesn't specifically pertain to transactions involving securities repurchase agreements."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. The Treasury rate refers to the yield on government securities, specifically U.S Treasury bonds, notes and bills. It's not directly related with repurchase agreement transactions as proposed by Das."}],"remember":["Repurchase Agreement (Repo) transactions involve selling securities with an agreement to repurchase them at a later date at a higher price, effectively acting as a short-term collateralized loan.","Repo Rate is the interest rate associated with repurchase agreements and is used by financial institutions for short-term borrowing and lending of funds.","Central banks use the Repo Rate as a monetary policy tool to manage liquidity and control the money supply in the economy.","LIBOR (London Interbank Offered Rate) is a benchmark interest rate used for interbank lending, not specifically for repurchase agreements.","Fed funds rate is the interest rate at which depository institutions lend reserve balances to other depository institutions overnight, not directly related to repo transactions.","Treasury rate refers to the yield on government securities like U.S. Treasury bonds, notes, and bills, and is not directly linked to repurchase agreements."]},{"id":"650","page":365,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The continuously compounded interest rate is 9.23%."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The continuously compounded interest rate is 8.33%."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The continuously compounded interest rate is 8.05%."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The continuously compounded interest rate is 7.97%."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Gamze Goc is an independent wealth advisor that focuses on providing investment and savings advice to professionals. She advised one of her clients to invest $10,000 for 5 years into a government’s national saving plan which pays a monthly interest of 8% per year. This rate is fixed regardless of the tenure of the investment. Since the client does not have an alternative option to invest his savings, he asked what interest rates he would earn if the rate was compounded continuously. Identify the most appropriate answer to the client’s inquiry."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The formula used to convert a rate that is compounded at a certain frequency into a continuously compounded rate is:"},{"type":"p","text":"Rc = m ∗ ln (1 + Rm )"},{"type":"pre","text":"m"},{"type":"p","text":"Where m is the compounding frequency, Rm is the compounded rate at m frequency And Rc is the continuously compounded rate"},{"type":"pre","text":"Rc = 12 × ln (1 + 01.208) = 0.07973"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"651","page":365,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The quarterly compounded rate is 15.6%."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The quarterly compounded rate is 16%."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The quarterly compounded rate is 16.3%."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The quarterly compounded rate is 17.1%."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Ahmed Hatti is an undergrad business and finance student at the University of Millennials. Along with his friend, he manages a small hypothetical fund from his dorm room. The fund consists of small investments from his colleagues and family. As a fund manager, he is also responsible for generating a quarterly income newsletter, which he has to email to all fund contributors. Recently, Hatti decided to invest a small portion of his fund into an interest-bearing account that quotes an interest rate of 16% compounded continuously. In order to add the interest rate into the quarterly newsletter, he must convert the continuously compounded rate into a quarterly compounded rate. Which of the following is the most appropriate conversion of the rate?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The formula used to convert a continuously compounded rate into a rate that is compounded at a"},{"type":"p","text":"certain frequency is:"},{"type":"p","text":"Rm = m {e Rc −1}"},{"type":"pre","text":"m"},{"type":"p","text":"Where"},{"type":"p","text":"m is the compounding frequency,"},{"type":"p","text":"Rm is the annual rate, compounded m times a year And Rc is the continuously compounded rate"},{"type":"pre","text":"0.16"},{"type":"p","text":"Rm = 4 (e 4 – 1) = 16.32%"}],"remember":["Continuous compounding is a method of calculating interest where interest is calculated and added to the principal continuously, rather than at specific intervals.","The formula for continuously compounded interest is: A = P ⋅ ert, where A is the amount of money accumulated after t years, including interest, P is the principal amount, r is the annual interest rate (in decimal form), and t is the time the money is invested for in years.","Compounding frequency refers to how often the interest is calculated and added to the principal amount. Common compounding frequencies include annually, semi-annually, quarterly, and monthly.","When converting a continuously compounded rate to a rate compounded at a specific frequency, the formula Rm = m {e Rc −1} is used, where Rm is the annual rate m compounded m times a year, Rc is the continuously compounded rate, and m is the compounding frequency."]},{"id":"653","page":367,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The future value of a zero-coupon is $1,656.0."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The future value of a zero-coupon is $1,828.0."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The future value of a zero-coupon is $1,877.6."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The future value of a zero-coupon is $1,912.0."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investor has invested $1,000 in a 7-year zero-coupon bond with continuous compounding. If the bond is quoted as 9% per year compounded continuously, then estimate the value of the investment at the end of 7 years."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Since the zero-coupon bond is compounded continuously, the future value of this 7-years 9% per annum bond is:"},{"type":"pre","text":"F V = Face value ∗ erate∗period\n      = 1000 ∗ e0.09∗7\n      = 1, 877.6"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"655","page":367,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Interpolation"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Duration"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Bootstrapping"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Calibration"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The process by which traders can use the quotes of treasury bills and coupon-bearing treasury bonds to derive a zero-coupon yield curve or spot curve is referred to as:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Bootstrapping is a financial method used to derive a zero-coupon yield curve, also known as a spot rate curve, from the rates and quotes of zero-coupon and coupon-bearing Treasury bonds. The term 'bootstrapping' refers to the process of 'pulling oneself up by one's bootstraps', which is a metaphor for achieving a complex task starting from basics without external help. In the context of finance, bootstrapping is a method of constructing a yield curve. The yield curve, in turn, is a graphical representation of the interest rates on debt for a range of maturities. It shows the yield an investor is expected to earn if he lends money for a given period of time. The spot rate curve or zero-coupon yield curve represents the yields of hypothetical zero-coupon bonds. Since they are not directly observable in the market, they are derived from the yields of coupon-bearing bonds, and this process is known as bootstrapping."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. Interpolation is a statistical method used to estimate values between two known values. While it can be used in the process of constructing a yield curve, it is not the specific process referred to in this question. Choice B is incorrect. Duration refers to the sensitivity of a bond's price to changes in interest rates. It does not refer to the process of constructing a zero-coupon yield curve or spot curve using treasury bills and bonds that bear coupons. Choice D is incorrect. Calibration involves adjusting model parameters within certain bounds until model outputs match observed data. Although calibration might be part of building financial models, it does not specifically refer to the construction of a zero-coupon yield curve or spot curve."}],"remember":["Zero-coupon bonds are bonds that do not pay periodic interest but are issued at a discount to their face value and redeemed at face value.","Spot rates are the yields on zero-coupon bonds maturing at different points in time.","The zero-coupon yield curve is used in various financial calculations, such as pricing fixed-income securities, valuing interest rate derivatives, and assessing the term structure of interest rates.","Bootstrapping is a common method used in fixed income markets to derive the zero-coupon yield curve.","The process of bootstrapping involves solving for the spot rates at different maturities by using the prices of coupon-bearing bonds and applying the law of one price."]},{"id":"656","page":368,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"6.5%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"7.2%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"8.6%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"9.3%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Every year, thousands of students in Turkey take the Certified Trader exam. The exam tests in detail the knowledge of students who are willing to join the banking sector. In last year’s exam, a question asked the students to calculate a 1-year forward rate 2 years from now. The question also provided the following table of zero spot rates p.a:"},{"type":"p","text":"Year Zero rates (per year)"},{"type":"pre","text":"1                  6%\n2                 6.5%\n3                 7.2%"},{"type":"p","text":"Using the information provided in the table, which of the following is the accurate 1-year forward rate 2 years from now?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The 1-year forward rate 2 years from now is calculated as:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Forward  Rate  =  Spot    Rateyear  3  +  (Spot  Rate year 3  − Spot  Rateyear  2)   ∗  (  Time2  )\n                                                                                           (Time3– Time2)\nForward  Rate  =  0.072   +  (0.072– 0.065)      ∗  ((3  2  2))  =  0.086  or  8.6%\n                                                         −"},{"type":"p","text":"Alternative Approach Let's start off by assuming that there are two people, each with $100 A chooses to invest his cash for 3 years straight at the 3-year spot rate. B opts to invest his cash for 2 years at the 2-year spot rate and then reinvest his proceeds for a further year at the 1-year forward rate at that point. In a fair market, both A and B should have the same amount of money at the end of year 3. What that means, therefore is that the 3-year spot rate is equal to the 2-year spot rate multiplied by the 1-year forward rate two years from today. In other words,"},{"type":"pre","text":"(1 + 3 − year spot)3 = (1 + 2 − year spot)2(1 + 1 − year forward)"},{"type":"p","text":"We should set the forward rate such that B ends with the same amount of money as A."},{"type":"pre","text":"1.0723 = 1.0652 × 1 − year forward rate"},{"type":"p","text":"1-year forward rate = 0.086"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"657","page":370,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Cash outflow of $25,000."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Cash inflow of $24,582."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Cash outflow of $24,500."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Cash outflow of $24,582."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Beijing Shipping Corp. enters into a forward rate agreement with Geneva Bank to receive a 7% fixed rate on the principal of $50 million based on a three-month rate beginning in six-month time. If the three-month rate in six-month time is 6.8%, then what is the cash inflow/outflow for the Beijing Shipping at the end of the sixth month?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The FRA’s payoff will take place in the ninth month. The net payoff will be the difference between the receipt of the fixed rate of 7% and the floating rate payment. As given in the question, if the floating rate is 6.8% in six months, so the payoff at the end of the ninth month is calculated as:"},{"type":"p","text":"Payoff = Principal × (Fixed rate – Floating rate) × Time"},{"type":"pre","text":"= 50,000,000 × (0.07– 0.068) × 0.25\n= 25,000"},{"type":"p","text":"Although any interest should be due at the end of the FRA period (e.g., 9 months in this question), the common practice is for the FRA to be settled at the beginning of the FRA period or rather as soon as the floating rate becomes known. Therefore, we can find the payoff at the end of the sixth month by discounting any payoffs for three months, with the floating rate as the discount rate."},{"type":"pre","text":"Payoff  =  $25, 000      =  $24, 582\n           (1 + 0.4068)"},{"type":"p","text":"Since the payoff is positive, Beijing Corp. will receive a cash inflow of $24,582 at the six-month point."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"660","page":371,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The price of the bond will increase by $0.95"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The price of the bond will decrease by $0.95"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The price of the bond will increase by $0.98"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The price of the bond will decrease by $0.98"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Hina Bibi is a fixed-income analyst at Vio Investment Company. She is responsible for analyzing the risk and return of a company’s portfolio of fixed income investments. She is analyzing the change in the price of a hypothetical 7-year bond with the face value of 100 and the price of 96.86. If the duration of the bond that she analyzing is 1.962, then which of the following options presents the accurate change in the price of the bond if the yield on the bond increases by 50 basis points?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The change in the price of a bond given the change in the yield can be predicted by duration. In the given question, where the price of the bond is 96.86, and the duration of the bond is 1.962, a 50 basis point increase in the yield will decrease the price of the bond by: Change in price = -Bond price * Duration * Change in yield Change in price = -96.86 * 1.962 * 0.005 = -0.950 Therefore, the price of the bond after the 50 basis point increase in yield is 96.86 – 0.950 = 95.91."}],"remember":["Duration measures the sensitivity of a bond's price to changes in interest rates.","A bond's price and yield have an inverse relationship -when yields increase, bond prices decrease, and vice versa.","Modified duration is a measure of a bond's sensitivity to changes in interest rates and is calculated as Duration / (1 + Yield).","Convexity is a measure of the curvature of the price-yield relationship of a bond and helps refine the estimate of the bond's price change given a change in yield.","For a bond with positive convexity, the actual price change will be slightly higher than what is predicted by duration alone when yields change."]},{"id":"662","page":371,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Duration"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Convexity"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Modified duration"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Concavity"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"There are different measures available that are used to measure the change in the price of the bond given the change in the yield curve. Which of the following measures is used for the purpose of estimating changes in bond prices if the changes in the yield curve are larger?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Convexity is a measure of the curvature in the relationship between bond prices and bond yields that demonstrates how the duration of a bond changes as the interest rate changes. This measure is used to assess the sensitivity of the price of a bond to changes in interest rates. Convexity is particularly useful when there are larger changes in interest rates, as it provides a more accurate estimate of bond price changes than duration alone. Convexity takes into account the effects of changes in the yield curve on bond prices, which can be significant when the changes in the yield curve are large. Therefore, convexity is the most appropriate measure to use when estimating changes in bond prices due to larger changes in the yield curve."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. Duration is a measure of the sensitivity of the price of a bond to changes in interest rates. However, it assumes that this relationship is linear and does not account for substantial changes in the yield curve."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. Modified duration, like duration, measures the price sensitivity of a bond to interest rate changes but it also assumes a linear relationship between bond prices and yields. It fails to capture the effects of larger shifts in the yield curve."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. Concavity isn't used as a metric in bond investing for predicting alterations in bond prices due to yield curve fluctuations. It's rather an attribute related to convexity which describes how the change in yield affects convexity itself."}],"remember":["Duration is a measure of the sensitivity of the price of a bond to changes in interest rates, assuming a linear relationship.","Modified duration adjusts the basic duration measure to account for changes in interest rates.","Convexity measures the curvature in the relationship between bond prices and bond yields, providing a more accurate estimate of bond price changes than duration alone.","Convexity is particularly useful when there are larger changes in interest rates, as it captures the effects of changes in the yield curve on bond prices.","Concavity is a related attribute to convexity, describing how the change in yield affects convexity itself."]},{"id":"663","page":373,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Expectation theory"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Market segmentation theory"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Liquidity preference theory"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"None of the above"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Fredrick Hessen is a senior professor in the department of macroeconomics at Welth Business School. In the current semester, his course focuses on interest rates and the term structure of interest rates. One day, he made the following comment: “There is no relationship between short-term, medium-term, and long-term interest rates. These interest rates are independently determined by the supply and demand in their specific bond market. For instance, the short-term interest rate is determined by the supply and demand of short-term bonds”."},{"type":"p","text":"Which of the following theories is associated with the professor’s comment?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Market Segmentation Theory is the correct answer. This theory posits that the markets for different maturity bonds are completely segmented and that the interest rates for each segment are determined by the supply and demand within that segment. This means that the interest rates for short-term, medium-term, and long-term bonds are determined independently of each other. This theory aligns with Professor Hessen's statement that there is no relationship between short-term, medium-term, and long-term interest rates and that these rates are independently determined by the supply and demand in their specific bond markets. The Market Segmentation Theory also explains why certain investors, such as pension funds and insurance companies, prefer bonds of a certain maturity and are unlikely to switch from one maturity to another based on liquidity considerations."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. The Expectation theory suggests that long-term interest rates are an average of expected future short-term rates, implying a direct relationship between short, medium and long term rates. This contradicts Professor Hessen's viewpoint which suggests these rates are independently determined."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The Liquidity preference theory posits that investors demand a premium for longer term bonds due to the increased risk associated with holding assets over a longer period of time. This implies an interrelationship between different term interest rates, which again contradicts Professor Hessen's viewpoint."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. As explained above, there exists a theory in economics - the Market Segmentation Theory - that aligns with Professor Hessen's viewpoint suggesting that different term interest rates are independently determined by their respective bond markets' supply and demand dynamics."}],"remember":["Expectation Theory: Suggests that long-term interest rates are an average of expected future short-term rates, implying a direct relationship between short, medium, and long-term rates.","Liquidity Preference Theory: Posits that investors demand a premium for longer-term bonds due to the increased risk associated with holding assets over a longer period of time.","Term Structure of Interest Rates: Refers to the relationship between short-term, medium-term, and long-term interest rates.","Segmented Markets: The concept that different segments of the bond market are isolated from each other, leading to independent determination of interest rates within each segment."]},{"id":"664","page":374,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Expectation theory"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Market segmentation theory"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Liquidity preference theory"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"None of the above"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Transactions worth billions of dollars depend on the shape of the zero rate curve. The shape of the zero curve has gained the attention of economists, mathematicians, and investors. Many theories exist that present their perspective about the shape of the zero curve. One of those theories suggests that investors are likely to invest their funds for a shorter period while borrowers are more willing to borrow the funds at long-term fixed rates. The theory also concludes that the forward rates are greater than the future spot rates, which justifies the empirical result that the yield curve tends to be upward sloping. Which of the following theories provides the above-mentioned conclusion?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Liquidity Preference Theory is the correct answer. This theory suggests that investors prefer to invest their funds for shorter periods, while borrowers are more inclined to borrow at long-term fixed rates. The theory also asserts that forward rates exceed future spot rates, providing an empirical explanation for the upward slope of the yield curve. This theory is based on the premise that investors require a premium, or a higher return, for holding long-term securities because they are considered riskier. The risk arises from the uncertainty about future interest rates and inflation. Therefore, to compensate for this risk, long-term rates are generally higher than short-term rates, resulting in an upward-sloping yield curve."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. The Expectation theory suggests that the yields on long-term bonds are an average of expected future short-term rates. It does not account for the preference of investors to invest their funds for shorter periods or borrowers' inclination to borrow at long-term fixed rates."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. The Market segmentation theory posits that financial markets are segmented and each segment (short, intermediate, and long term) has its own supply and demand dynamics influencing interest rates. This theory does not explain the preference of investors or borrowers as described in the question."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. As explained above, the description provided in the question matches with Liquidity preference theory which makes option D - 'None of the above' incorrect."}],"remember":["Yield Curve: A graphical representation of the relationship between the interest rates (or yields) and the time to maturity of debt securities.","Forward Rates: The interest rates implied by current interest rates and expected future interest rates.","Spot Rates: The interest rates applicable to a specific period in the future.","Risk Premium: The additional return that investors require for holding a risky asset compared to a risk-free asset.","Interest Rate Risk: The risk that arises from changes in interest rates impacting the value of fixed-income securities."]},{"id":"3533","page":376,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1375"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1198"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1319"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1288"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Stock IIK is currently selling for $80. The 28 analysts offering 12-month price targets for IIK have a median target of $91. Given that the stock reaches the median target of $91 in 12 months, what is the continuously compounded return of this asset?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"Future value = Present Value × eRT"},{"type":"p","text":"Where R is the continuously compounded rate of interest, and T is the time to maturity."},{"type":"pre","text":"91 = 80eR∗1\n         91\n⇒ eR  =  80\n⇒ R = ln ( 8910)\n= 0.12883"},{"type":"p","text":"Alternatively,"},{"type":"p","text":"Holding period return = ST −1"},{"type":"pre","text":"          S0\n          $91\n=         $80  −  1\n= 1.1375 − 1 = 13.75%"},{"type":"p","text":"Continuous Return = ln(1 + i) = ln(1 + 13.75%) = 0.12883"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3534","page":377,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"6%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"7.9%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"5.4%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"5.9%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investor invested $154,856 into a mutual fund 5 years ago. If the investment is now worth $201,694, what is the compound annual growth rate of the investment?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Compound annual growth rate (CAGR) = (Ending value/Beginning value)1/n = (201,594/154,856)1/5 - 1 = 5.4%"}],"remember":["Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR) is a useful measure of an investment's annual growth rate over time, taking into account the effect of compounding.","CAGR is often used to compare the returns on different investments over the same period of time.","It is important to note that CAGR assumes that the investment has been compounding steadily over the entire period.","CAGR is a geometric progression ratio that provides a constant rate of return over the time period being measured.","Investors use CAGR to determine the annual growth rate needed to reach a future value from an initial investment."]},{"id":"3535","page":378,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"10.375%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"10.25%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"10.5%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"10.42%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A bank advertises that it pays an annual interest of 10% with semi-annual compounding on its savings account. What is the effective annual rate?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"                Annual rate   Compounding   frequency\nE AR  =  (1  +               )                         −  1\n                Compounding frequency\n      = (1 + 10%/2)2 − 1 = 0.1025 = 10.25%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3536","page":379,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"43.10%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"28.00%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"51.08%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"25.54%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The price of a stock increases from $24 to $40 in two years. What is the continuously compounded annual return for the stock?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The continuously compounded 2-year return = ln(40/24) = 51.08% Annually compounded rate of return = (51.08% / 2) = 25.54%"}],"remember":["Continuous compounding is a method of calculating interest where the interest is added to the principal balance continuously, rather than at specific intervals.","The continuously compounded rate of return can be calculated using the formula: ln(Ending Value / Beginning Value).","Annual compounding is another method of calculating interest where the interest is added to the principal balance once a year.","To convert a continuously compounded rate to an annually compounded rate, you can divide the continuously compounded rate by the number of compounding periods in a year."]},{"id":"3537","page":380,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$200,321"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$216,657"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$202,149"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$217,985"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"In order to have liquid cash at hand, a company always keeps $200,000 in its bank account. The stated annual interest rate quoted by the bank is 8%. Assuming that compounding is done continuously and there have been no withdrawals and additions, what is the balance in the company's bank account after one year?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Using the formula for the future value with continuous compounding (N is the number of years in the expression)"},{"type":"pre","text":"FVN = $200 ,000e0.08(1) = $216, 657.41"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3538","page":381,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.76%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"9.12%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.73%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"8.76%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Jose Calzon currently has $5,040.11 in his bank account. If he plans to buy a car for $5,500 next year, what is the annual interest rate (compounded monthly), that a bank must pay so that James receives a sum of $5,500 next year?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Interest rate can also be considered as the required rate of return. In the above case, James"},{"type":"p","text":"wants his $5,040.11 to grow to $5,500. The rate required to achieve this return can be calculated"},{"type":"p","text":"as under: Amount deposited today × (1 + Rate of interest) = Amount next year"},{"type":"pre","text":"                       Amount  dneepxtosyietaerd−\nRate  of  interest  =  Amount                      1"},{"type":"p","text":"= ( $$55,0,54000.1)1 − 1 = 0.0912 or 9.12%"},{"type":"p","text":"To turn the annual interest rate into a monthly rate, we use the relationship:"},{"type":"pre","text":"1 + i = (1 +           im       m\n                       m\n                               )\n⇒                      1.0912  =   (1 +  i12   12\n                                         12   )\n                                          1\n⇒ m12 = [1.091212 − 1] × 12 = 0.08759"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3539","page":382,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$79,670"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$55,045"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$56,743"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$68,856"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investor received $100,000 after five years from a certificate of deposit which paid him an interest of 12% with monthly compounding. What is the sum deposited by the investor at the beginning of the 5 years?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Given an initial investment of A that earns an annual rate R, compounded m times a year for a"},{"type":"p","text":"total of n years, then we can compute the future value, FV, as follows:"},{"type":"p","text":"FV = A[1 + R ]m×n"},{"type":"pre","text":"m"},{"type":"p","text":"100, 000 = A[1 + 01.212]60"},{"type":"p","text":"A = $55,045"}],"remember":["Compound Interest: Compound interest is the interest calculated on the initial principal, which also includes all of the accumulated interest from previous periods on a deposit or loan.","Effective Annual Rate (EAR): The effective annual rate is the rate of interest that is actually earned or paid on an investment, loan, or other financial product due to the result of compounding over a given time period.","Time Value of Money: The concept that a dollar today is worth more than a dollar in the future due to its potential earning capacity is known as the time value of money.","Present Value: The present value is the current worth of a future sum of money or stream of cash flows given a specified rate of return."]},{"id":"3540","page":382,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"102.48"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"106.74"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"103.56"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"104.2"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A 3-year bond with a face value of $100 offers a 7% coupon rate with interest paid annually. Assuming the following sequence of spot rates, the price of the bond is closest to:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Time to Maturity            Spot Rate (%)\n           1                         4\n           2                         5\n           3                        5.5"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The price of a bond is the present value of its future cash flows, which in this case are the coupon payments and the face value return at maturity. Given the coupon rate of 7% and the face value of 100, the annual coupon payment is 7 (100) = 7 dollars. The spot rate for each year is given in the table. To find the present value of the cash flows, we can use the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"PV  =     C      +  (1  C  r)2  +  (1  C  r)3  +  (1  F  r)3\n       (1 + r)1         +              +              +"},{"type":"p","text":"Where:"},{"type":"p","text":"C = annual coupon payment F = face value r = spot rate Plugging in the values we get:"},{"type":"pre","text":"price = (1 +70.04)1+ (1 +70.05)2+ (1 +100.7055)3= 104.20"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the price of the bond is closest to $104.20$"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3541","page":383,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1115.3"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"995.65"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"1001.8"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"998.51"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An analyst has gathered the following estimated series of spot rates for a developing country:"},{"type":"pre","text":"0.5-year: 2%\n1-year: 3%\n1.5-year: 3.55%\n2-year: 4%\n2.5-year: 4.5%\n3-year: 5%\n3.5-year: 5.45%"},{"type":"p","text":"Given that the information is accurate, what is the price of a 3-year, 1,000 face value, 5% annual coupon paying bond?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The general approach to bond valuation is to utilize a series of spot rates to reflect the timing of future cash flows. (50/1.03) + (50/1.042) + (1,050/1.053) = 1,001.80"}],"remember":["Bond valuation involves calculating the present value of all future cash flows generated by the bond.","Spot rates are the yields-to-maturity on zero-coupon bonds maturing at different points in time.","The spot rate curve is used to discount each cash flow of a bond to determine its present value.","Coupon payments are typically fixed and paid semi-annually or annually, depending on the bond's terms.","Yield to maturity (YTM) is the total return anticipated on a bond if it is held until it matures."]},{"id":"3542","page":385,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"5%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"4.8%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"14.24%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"8.73%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The 2-period spot rate, S2 is 9%, and the 1-period spot rate, S1 is 4%. Calculate the forward rate for one period, one period from now, f1,1."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"(1 + S2)2 = (1 + S1)(1 + f1,1) f1,1 = [(1.09)2/1.04] -1 = 14.24%"},{"type":"p","text":"Alternatively, we can denote this as:"},{"type":"pre","text":"F  =  V2\n      V1"},{"type":"p","text":"Where V1 is the value to which one dollar grows by time T1 V2 is the value to which one dollar grows by time T2 In this case,"},{"type":"pre","text":"F  =  V2  =  1.092   1.1424\n      V1     1.04 ="},{"type":"p","text":"So that F = 1.1424 − 1 = 14.24%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3543","page":386,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"4%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"12.5%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"4.2%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"8.42%"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"If the current 1-year spot rate is 3%, the 1-year forward rate one year from today ( f1,1) is 4%, and the 1-year forward rate two years from today ( f2,1) is 5%, then the 3-year spot rate is closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"There is a connection between spot rates and forward rates. The accumulation amount at time k of an investment of $1 at time t=0 is given by (1 + sk)k. If we had agreed to invest this amount at time k for n-k years, then the accumulation at time n would be: (1 + sk)k. (1 + f[k,n])"},{"type":"p","text":"We are also aware that $1 invested at time 0 for n years will be (1 + sn)n"},{"type":"pre","text":"⇒ (1 + s k)k. (1 + f[k,n]) = (1 + sn)n\n                        (1  + sn)n\n⇒  (1  + f[k,n])n−k  =  (1  + sk)k"},{"type":"p","text":"For a one-period rate, fk = fk,1 . Also, define f0 = s1 . Using the same arguments,"},{"type":"pre","text":"⇒ (1 + sn)n = (1 + f0) (1 + f1) (1 + f2) … (1 + fn−1)"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore,"},{"type":"pre","text":"(1 + s3)3 = [(1.03)(1.04)(1.05)]\n                                                                                          1\n             ⇒ s3 = [(1.03)(1.04)(1.05)] 3 − 1 = 3.997%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3545","page":387,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 114.68"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 122.13"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 123.78"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 117.68"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A corporate bond has the following characteristics:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Price: USD 106.50\nCoupon rate: 5%\nDuration: 7.5 years\nConvexity: 101"},{"type":"p","text":"If the credit spreads narrow by 175 basis points, then what will be the price of the bond?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The change in bond's price resulting from the change in the yield can be estimated using the following formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"ΔB  =  −DBΔy        +  1  CB(Δy)2\n                       2"},{"type":"p","text":"Where ΔB = The change in the bond's price resulting from the change in the yield. Δy = The change in the bond's yield. B = the price of the bond. C = the convexity of the bond. D = the duration of the bond. From the information given in the question,"},{"type":"pre","text":"ΔB  =  −7.5  ×  106.50 ×  (−0.0175)  +  1  ×  101   ×  106.50  ×  (− 0.0175)2  =  15.62522\n                                        2"},{"type":"p","text":"The change in bond's price is positive so that the new price off the bond is:"},{"type":"pre","text":"15.62522 + 106.50 = 122.12522 ≈ 122.13"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3548","page":388,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"2.45 years"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.65 years"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2.30 years"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2.25 years"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"You have been provided the following information on a bond:"},{"type":"p","text":"Period PV of cash flow (USD Mn)"},{"type":"pre","text":"1                     3.7\n2                     4.9\n3                    22.3"},{"type":"p","text":"If the yield to maturity is 6%, then what is the modified duration of the bond?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Macaulay Duration = (1*3.7/30.9) + (2*4.9/30.9) + (3*22.3/30.9) = 2.60 years Modified Duration = Macaulay Duration / (1 + YTM) = 2.60 / 1.06 = 2.45 years"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3551","page":389,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.97"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"7.8"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"3.62"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"3.73"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A 4-year, 5% annual-pay bond has a face value of $1,000. The interest rate stands at 3% per annum. Determine the approximate modified duration?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"Modified     duration   =  Macaulay duration\n                               1     +  y\n                                        m"},{"type":"p","text":"Where y is the yield and m is the compounding frequency per annum. We first calculate the Macaulay duration:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Macaulay     duration  =       ∑nt=1 PV(Ct)T  Bond\n                           Market Price of"},{"type":"p","text":"Where PV(Ct) is the present value of coupon payments at time t, and T is the time to maturity"},{"type":"pre","text":"Price  of  the  bond =   50 +   50 + 50 +             1050   =  1074.34\n                        1.031  1.032 1.033            1.034"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"Macaulay  duration  =  10714.34×     50 +   10724.34×   50 +  10734.34×       50 +  10744.34×  1050    3.73412\n                                    1.03 1             1.032                 1.033             1.034="},{"type":"p","text":"And so the modified duration is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Modified     duration   =  Macaulay duration\n                                   1+   y\n                           3.73412      m\n                        =  1+  0.03\n                                1\n                        = 3.62535"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3552","page":390,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"-19.41%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"-24.90%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"-1.62%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"-4.51%"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A bond has a duration of 10.62 and a convexity of 91.46. For a 200 bps increase in yield, what is the bond's approximate percentage price change?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The estimated price change = -(Duration)*(Change in yield) + (1/2)*(Convexity)*(Change in yield)2 = -10.62*0.02 + 0.5*91.46*0.022 = -19.41%"}],"remember":["Duration measures the sensitivity of a bond's price to changes in interest rates.","Convexity is a measure of the curvature of the price-yield relationship of a bond and helps refine the estimate of price changes calculated using duration.","For small changes in interest rates, convexity helps improve the accuracy of the estimated percentage price change.","A positive convexity means that the bond's price increases at an increasing rate as yields decrease, and vice versa.","Convexity is especially important when interest rates change significantly or when the bond has a long maturity."]},{"id":"3553","page":391,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"-2.65%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.83%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"-2.80%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2.86%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A 9% bond has a full price of $905 and a YTM of 10%. Estimate the percentage change in the full price of the bond for a 30 basis point increase in YTM, assuming the bond's modified duration is 9.42, and its convexity is 68.33."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Expected change in bond's price = ΔP/P = -Dmod * Δy + 0.5 * C * Δy2 Duration effect = -Dmod * Δy = -9.42 * 0.003 = 0.02826 = -2.826% Convexity effect = 0.5 * C * Δy2 = 0.5 * 68.33 * 0.0032 = 0.000307 = 0.0307% Expected change in bond's price = (-0.02826 + 0.000307) = -2.79530%"}],"remember":["Modified duration measures the percentage change in the bond's price for a 1% change in yield to maturity.","Convexity is a measure of the curvature in the relationship between bond prices and bond yields.","Convexity helps to refine the estimate of the percentage change in bond price when there are large changes in interest rates.","When interest rates rise, bond prices fall and vice versa.","Duration and convexity are important risk measures used in fixed income portfolio management."]},{"id":"3554","page":392,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$3,988"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$3,960"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$3,970"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$3,368"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A bond valued at $200,000 has a duration of 8 and a convexity of 20. Assuming that the bond's spread relative to the benchmark curve increases by 25 basis points due to a credit downgrade, then what is the approximate change in the bond's market value?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Price Change = (-Duration * Yield change) + (0.5 * Convexity * Yield change2)) Price change = (−8 ∗ 0.0025) + (0.5 ∗ 20 ∗ 0.00252) = −1.99% The bond's value will fall by approximately 1.990% * 200,000 = $3,988."}],"remember":["Duration measures the sensitivity of a bond's price to changes in interest rates.","Convexity measures the curvature of the price-yield relationship of a bond.","An increase in spread relative to the benchmark curve indicates a higher credit risk for the bond.","Price change calculations using duration and convexity help estimate the impact of interest rate changes on bond values."]},{"id":"4821","page":392,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"We can forecast future interest rates by looking at the term structure of interest rates since the return on a long-term bond is, in essence, the average return on short-term bonds over the same period."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"We can forecast future interest rates by looking at past returns on similar instruments."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"We can forecast future interest rates by looking at the behavior of the stock market."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"We can forecast future interest rates by looking at the term structure of interest rates since the return on a short-term bond is essentially the average return on long-term bonds over the same period."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The expectations hypothesis (theory) states that:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Expectations Hypothesis, also known as the Pure Expectations Theory, posits that the return on a long-term bond is essentially the average return on short-term bonds over the same period. This theory asserts that expected future spot rates of interest are equal to the forward rates that can be calculated today. In other words, the forward rates are unbiased predictors for making expectations of future spot rates. Therefore, we can forecast future interest rates by looking at the term structure of interest rates. The term structure of interest rates, also known as the yield curve, is a graphical representation that shows the relationship between interest rates and the time to maturity of a debt for a given borrower in a given currency. The term structure reflects expectations about future interest rates, inflation rates, and real rates of return. Hence, by analyzing the term structure of interest rates, we can derive insights into future interest rate movements."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. The Expectations Hypothesis does not suggest that future interest rates can be forecasted by looking at past returns on similar instruments. This theory posits that the expected future interest rates are reflected in the term structure of current interest rates, not past returns."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. According to the Expectations Hypothesis, future interest rates are not predicted based on the behavior of the stock market. The theory focuses on bond markets and their term structures for predicting future interest rates."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. The statement contradicts the essence of Expectations Hypothesis which suggests that long-term bond yields are an average of expected short-term yields and not vice versa."}],"remember":["The Expectations Hypothesis is based on the idea that the term structure of interest rates reflects market expectations about future interest rates.","Forward rates are unbiased predictors of future spot rates according to the Expectations Hypothesis.","The term structure of interest rates, also known as the yield curve, is a crucial tool for analyzing interest rate movements and expectations.","Market participants use the yield curve to make decisions about borrowing, lending, and investing based on their expectations of future interest rates."]},{"id":"4822","page":394,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"36.14%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"12.04%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"11.04%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"10.83%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The annual yield of a one-year government bond is 10% and the expected yield on a one-year bond starting one year from now is 11.0%. The expected yield on a one-year bond starting two years from now is 11.5%. According to the expectations hypothesis, what would be the annual yield of a three-year government bond?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"According to the Expectations Theory, the forward rates are unbiased predictors for making expectations of future spot rates. We can therefore forecast future interest rates by looking at the term structure of interest rates since the return on a long-term bond is in essence the average return on short-term bonds over the same period."},{"type":"pre","text":"(1 + r3)3 = (1 + r1)(1 + f1,1)(1 + f2,1)\n(1 + r3)3 = (1 + 0.1)(1 + 0.11)(1 + 0.115)\n    1 + r3 = 1.1083\n         r3 = 10.83%"},{"type":"p","text":"Note: We can as well simply find the average of the short-term rates: Three-year rate = (10%+113%+11.5%=) 10.83%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4825","page":394,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1.98%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.61%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"1.31%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2.25%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Four U.S. T-bonds currently on the market have the following characteristics:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Price per  Coupon (paid   Maturity  Semiannual\n$100       semiannually)  (yrs.)    period"},{"type":"p","text":"par value"},{"type":"pre","text":"$101.50           5.0%        0.5          1\n                 5.25%        1.0          2\n$102.60          5.75%        1.5          3\n                 6.20%        2.0          4"},{"type":"p","text":"$103.15"},{"type":"p","text":"$103.95"},{"type":"p","text":"Determine the one-year spot rate via the bootstrapping method, assuming semiannual compounding."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Bootstrapping is the process of carving out spot rates from the market prices of a set of coupon-paying bonds. The spot rates are determined in a consequential manner: after obtaining the first spot rate, say, for six months, we use that to obtain the spot rate for one year. Then we can use the six-month and one-year spot rates to obtain the one and a half-year spot rate, and so on."},{"type":"p","text":"We know that the price of a bond is the discounted value of all of its future cashflows."},{"type":"p","text":"For the six month T-bond, therefore,"},{"type":"pre","text":"                            (100 + 5/2)\n               101.5 = (1 + Z20.5)1\n101.5  [(1  +  Z0.5   1  =  $102.5\n                2\n                     )]\n       (1 +    Z0.5   1  =  102.5\n                2           101.5\n                     )\n       (1   +  Z0.5   1  =  1.0099\n                2\n                     )\n               Z0.5 = 0.0099 × 2 = 1.98%"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, the six-month spot rate is 1.98% with semiannual compounding. For the 1-year T-bond,"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                          $100 + 5.25\n  5.25/2      +  $100 +         5.25\n(1 + z02.5)1                     2"},{"type":"p","text":"102.6 ="},{"type":"pre","text":"                    Z1   2\n         (1      +  2\n                        )\n$ 5.25   +   $100 +         $ 5.25\n   2                          2"},{"type":"p","text":"102.6 ="},{"type":"pre","text":"                    (1 + 0.02198)1                  Z1   2\n                                         (1 +       2\n                                                        )\n102.6            =  $2.6 +  $102.63\n                            (1    +  Z1   2\n                                     2\n                                         )\n100(1 +  Z1   2  =  $102.63\n         2\n             )\n(1 +     Z1   2  =  1.0263\n         2\n             )\n(1 +     Z2z11)  =  √1.0263  =    1.0131\n           2     =  0.0131\n             z1 = 2.61%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4826","page":396,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It’s based on the average borrowing rates across a larger collection of banks."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It’s easier to compute."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It’s based on actual observable transactions, not estimates."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It incorporates a built-in hedge against default risk."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The main advantage of the secured overnight financing rate (SOFR) over LIBOR is that:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR) is a benchmark interest rate for dollar-denominated derivatives and loans, which is based on actual observable transactions. Unlike LIBOR, which is based on estimates provided by a panel of banks, SOFR is derived from the Treasury repurchase market, which is a highly liquid and active market. This means that SOFR is based on a large volume of actual transactions, making it a more reliable and transparent benchmark. The use of actual transaction data reduces the risk of manipulation that was a concern with LIBOR, which was based on estimates rather than actual transactions. Therefore, the main advantage of SOFR over LIBOR is its basis on actual observable transactions, not estimates."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. While it's true that SOFR is based on a larger collection of banks, this isn't the primary advantage of SOFR over LIBOR. The main advantage lies in its basis on actual observable transactions rather than estimates."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. The ease of computation isn't the primary reason for the transition from LIBOR to SOFR. Both rates involve complex calculations and require sophisticated financial knowledge to understand and compute. Choice D is incorrect. Although SOFR incorporates some risk measures, it does not provide a built-in hedge against default risk. Its main advantage over LIBOR lies in its transparency and reliability as it's based on actual observable transactions."}],"remember":["LIBOR (London Interbank Offered Rate) is a benchmark interest rate that was commonly used for short-term loans and derivatives. However, due to concerns about its reliability and susceptibility to manipulation, global regulators have been pushing for its replacement with alternative rates like SOFR.","SOFR (Secured Overnight Financing Rate) is based on the overnight Treasury repurchase market, where participants borrow and lend cash overnight using US Treasury securities as collateral. This market is considered more transparent and less prone to manipulation compared to the interbank lending market that LIBOR is based on.","Transitioning from LIBOR to alternative rates like SOFR involves significant operational and contractual challenges for financial institutions, as many existing contracts and financial products are tied to LIBOR. Proper planning and communication are essential for a smooth transition.","SOFR is just one of the alternative reference rates being considered globally to replace LIBOR. Other rates include the Sterling Overnight Index Average (SONIA) in the UK and the Euro Short-Term Rate (€STR) in the Eurozone."]},{"id":"4902","page":398,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1.6667"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"1.626"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"1.3333"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"1.3008"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a bond whose current price is USD 150 with a cash flow in one year providing a present value of USD 50 and a cash flow in two years providing a present value of USD 100. Suppose further that a yield of 5% with semi-annual compounding applies on the bond. What is the value of modified duration?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We that know that,"},{"type":"pre","text":"Modified  duration     =     ⎡   Maca(u1l+ay ynd)uratio⎥⎥⎦⎤n\n                             ⎢⎢\n                             ⎣"},{"type":"p","text":"We, therefore, start by calculating the Macaulay duration:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Macaulay  duration  =    n   ti  [  PV (Cti )]\n                                        P\n                       ∑\n                       i =1\n                       50                 100\n                    =  150   ×      1  +  150   ×  2  =  1.6667"},{"type":"p","text":"Where P V (Cti) = present value of cash flows at time ti and P is the price of the bond. Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"Modified duration = 1.6667 = 1.62604\n                                (1 + 0.205)"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4904","page":399,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The position decreases by $30."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The position increases by $60."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The position increases by $30."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The position does not change."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a portfolio that has a bond position worth USD 10,000. Suppose the position has a modified duration of 2 years and a convexity of 30. Assume that the term structure is flat. By how much does the value of the position change if interest rates increase by 15 basis points?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Change in bond's price = Duration effect + Convexity effect"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                    = [– Duration × Price × Change in yield]\n+[  1  ×  Convexity  ×  Price  ×  (Change  in   yield)2]"},{"type":"p","text":"2"},{"type":"pre","text":"= (−2 ×   10, 000  ×  0.0015)  +  (  1  × 30 ×  10, 000 ×                     0.00152)\n                                     2"},{"type":"p","text":"= −29.665 ≈ −30"},{"type":"p","text":"This means that, with every increase in interest rates of 15 basis points, the bond's price will decrease by $30"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4905","page":400,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"11.27%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.88%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"6.34%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"1.08%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Suppose the zero-coupon interest rates (semi-annually compounded) for maturities of 0.5, 1.0, and 1.5 years are 2.5%, 3.0% and 3.5%, respectively. Consider a USD 1,000 face value, two-year bond that currently trades at USD 1,060 and pays coupons at a rate of 6% per year every six months. If the two-year zero-coupon interest rate is R, what is the value of R?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"R can be solved using the following equation:"},{"type":"pre","text":"   30 +             30          +       30             + 1030         = 1, 060\n1  +  0.025  (1 + 0.0230)2              (1 + 0.2035)3  (1  +   R   4\n        2                                                      2\n                                                                  )\n             ⇒  1030            = 1, 060 − 87.22 = 972.78\n                (1  +  R   4\n                       2\n                          )\n                                             1\n                          R        ( 1, 030) 4\n             ⇒  (1  +     2  )  =   972.78      =      1.0144"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"R = 0.0144 × 2 = 0.0288 = 2.88%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4908","page":401,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 4.94 is paid out"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 12.35 is received"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 4.88 is paid out"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 4.94 is received"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"In an FRA, an annual rate of 2% will be received, in exchange for a 6-month LIBOR to be paid on a principal of USD 2,000 for a six-month period starting in 15 months. If the six-month forward rate in 15 months is 2.5% per annum, what is the settlement on the FRA?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The settlement in 15 months is given by,"},{"type":"p","text":"(Fixed rate − Forward rate) × Term of the FRA × Principal"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                              1+  forward rate\n                                                         n\n                                          6\n  (0.02                       − 0.025) ×  12  × 2, 000\n=                                                     = −4.9383\n                              1+  0.025\n                                    2"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, USD 4.94 will be paid."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4909","page":402,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1.0"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.6312"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2.7891"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2.7206"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A three-year bond with a face value of USD 1,000 pays coupons of 8% per annum. The yield on the bond is 6% compounded annually. What is the Macaulay duration?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We first calculate the present value of cash flows as follows,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P  =   80 +                      80 +  1080           =  1, 053.46\n      1.061                     1.062  1.063"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, the Macaulay duration is given by,"},{"type":"pre","text":"Macaulay  Duration  =    n                ti  [   P V (Ct )  ]\n                                                 ∑ P V (Ct)\n                       ∑                  80                    80            1080\n                       i=1\n                    = 1, 01.50631.46× 1 + 1, 10.50632.46× 2 + 1,10.50633.46× 3\n                    = 2.7891"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4910","page":403,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"2.6312"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.7206"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2.7891"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"1.0"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A three-year bond with a face value of USD 1,000 pays coupons of 8% per annum. The yield on the bond is 6% compounded annually. What is the Modified duration?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We first calculate the present value of cash flows as follows,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P  =                       80 +    80 +  1080           =  1, 053.46\n                          1.061   1.062  1.063"},{"type":"p","text":"The Macaulay duration is given by,"},{"type":"pre","text":"Macaulay  Duration                  =    n  ti  [   P V (Ct )  ]\n                                                   ∑ P V (Ct)\n                                       ∑    80                    80            1080\n                                       i=1\n                                    = 1, 10.50631.46× 1 + 1, 01.50632.46× 2 + 1,10.50633.46× 3\n                                    = 2.7891"},{"type":"p","text":"And thus, the modified duration is given by"},{"type":"pre","text":"Modified Duration = 21..708691= 2.6312"}],"remember":[]}]);
